---
<p>A relational database keeps an application's facts in tables and lets it ask questions across them. A shop can record customers, orders and products separately, then ask which orders Mina placed, what each contains and what she still owes. The same stored facts answer several questions.</p>
<p>The database also manages changes to those facts. Two customers may try to buy the last mug at once. A purchase may fail halfway through. A server may crash after accepting an order but before replying. Tables give those problems a place to be expressed; constraints and transactions provide tools for handling them. Choosing the rules and the work that belongs together remains part of designing the application.</p>
<p>Every example follows a small shop selling tea and tea things. Mina Shah buys one blue mug and two packets of green tea in order 103. We will first read the records after that purchase, then go back to watch it happen. The drawings use invented shop data; timings and physical storage layouts are not implied.</p>

<section aria-labelledby="tables">
  <h2 id="tables">Tables and rows</h2>
  <p>A <em>table</em> is a named collection of rows. Each <em>row</em> records one fact of a particular kind, using named <em>columns</em>. In <code>customers</code>, a row records a customer and the columns give their identifier and name. In <code>orders</code>, a row records an order, including which customer placed it.</p>
  <p>Start at order 103 in the drawing. Follow its customer identifier to Mina, then follow its order lines to the products.</p>
  <ShopFigure kind="tables" number={1} />
  <p>The value <code>1</code> in order 103's <code>customer_id</code> refers to Mina's customer row. It is a reference, not a copy of her name. Order 101 points to her too. If her name changes, both orders still identify the same customer.</p>
  <p>A <em>key</em> identifies a row. The customer identifier is a key of <code>customers</code>; the order identifier is a key of <code>orders</code>. An order line needs two columns together: <code>order_id</code> and <code>line_no</code>. Line 1 of order 103 is different from line 1 of order 101. A key need not be a number, and its purpose is identity, not counting rows.</p>
  <p>Row position is no substitute. The database can return rows in a different order, and a name can change or be shared by two people. When the order of a result matters, the question must ask for it explicitly.</p>
  <p>The tables, their columns, value types and rules form the database's <em>schema</em>. A <em>relation</em> is the mathematical idea behind a table; “relational” does not merely mean that tables have links. SQL tables are its practical form, with features such as duplicate query rows and missing values that we will meet below.</p>
  <p>Choosing what one row should mean is the first design decision. A product's current price and the price agreed on an earlier order line are different facts, even if both happen to say 20.00 today. <a href="/learn/databases/relational/modelling/">Relational data modelling</a> develops those choices through changes to this shop.</p>
  <h3 id="values">Values and missing data</h3>
  <p>The characters on a page can hide differences in meaning. A quantity of <code>2</code> counts packets; <code>4.50</code> is an amount per packet; <code>P3</code> identifies a product. Order 103's <code>placed_at</code> records a moment, 20 October 2026 at 10:15 UTC. The schema gives each column a <em>data type</em>, defining the values it can hold and the operations available on them.</p>
  <p>Zero is a value. A stock of zero means there are no units available. A missing value needs a different interpretation: a nullable dispatch time might mean “not dispatched yet”. SQL calls a missing value <code>NULL</code>. It does not behave like a zero or an empty string. The shop must decide which meanings are allowed before choosing a representation; <a href="/learn/databases/relational/values/">Values, missing data and equality</a> follows the consequences.</p>
  <p>Order 103 has no payment row. That is an absence from another table, not a payment with a <code>NULL</code> amount. It remains pending, with 29.00 due. Keeping the distinction will matter when we ask for unpaid orders.</p>
  <h3 id="constraints">Constraints</h3>
  <p>A <em>constraint</em> is a rule the database enforces on the data it accepts. A primary key makes the chosen identity unique and non-missing. A foreign key requires a reference to name an existing row. A check constraint can require stock to stay at or above zero.</p>
  <div class="table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="Changes accepted or refused by the shop schema"><table><caption>The rule follows the data, whichever caller changes it</caption><thead><tr><th>Attempted change</th><th>Result</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Change P7's price from 20.00 to 22.00</td><td>Accepted: a non-negative price</td></tr><tr><td>Add another order with <code>order_id = 103</code></td><td>Refused: that identity already exists</td></tr><tr><td>Add an order for customer 99</td><td>Refused: there is no customer 99</td></tr><tr><td>Set P7's stock to −1</td><td>Refused: stock must not be negative</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
  <p>The price change is a separate experiment, not a change to our starting records. Notice what the constraints do not say: an order must have all its intended lines, and accepting it must allocate enough stock. Those rules involve several steps. <a href="/learn/databases/relational/constraints/">Constraints and data integrity</a> explains which rules a schema can enforce directly and which need a transaction protocol.</p>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="questions">
  <h2 id="questions">Asking questions of the same facts</h2>
  <p><em>SQL</em>, pronounced “S-Q-L” or “sequel”, is the language used to define, read and change these tables. A <code>SELECT</code> asks for a result. This one asks for Mina's orders:</p>
  <Sql label="Select Mina's orders" code={`SELECT order_id, status
FROM orders
WHERE customer_id = 1
ORDER BY order_id;`} />
  <p><code>FROM orders</code> names the input. <code>WHERE</code> keeps rows whose customer identifier is 1. <code>SELECT</code> names the columns to return, and <code>ORDER BY</code> puts the answer in order of identifier. The result has two rows: 101, paid; and 103, pending. Reading does not remove or change either order.</p>
  <p>A <em>join</em> pairs rows from tables according to a condition. For each line of order 103, the next query finds the product whose identifier matches the line's <code>product_id</code>. Watch each match add a row to the result.</p>
  <Sql label="Pair order 103's lines with product names" code={`SELECT i.order_id, i.line_no, p.name,
       i.quantity, i.unit_price
FROM order_items AS i
JOIN products AS p ON p.product_id = i.product_id
WHERE i.order_id = 103
ORDER BY i.line_no;`} />
  <ShopFigure kind="join" number={2} />
  <p>The short names <code>i</code> and <code>p</code> identify the two input tables. <code>i.unit_price</code> comes from the order line; <code>p.name</code> comes from the product. The result has its own columns and its own row meaning: one line together with its product name.</p>
  <p>Order 103 appears twice because it has two lines. There are still only three stored orders in the shop. Counting these result rows would count lines, not orders. Asking how much is still due also requires bringing in payments, which can themselves have several rows per order. <a href="/learn/databases/relational/query-results/">Joins and the meaning of a result row</a> builds that report without counting the same money twice.</p>
  <p>The SQL describes the answer, not a prescribed physical search. Pairing by product identifier does not require the server to try every possible pair. How it finds the rows is a separate question, to which we will return.</p>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="purchase">
  <h2 id="purchase">Making a purchase</h2>
  <p>Go back to just before order 103. P7 has one mug in stock, P3 has twelve packets of tea, and order 103 does not yet exist. Mina wants the mug and two teas. Accepting her purchase must record the order and its two lines, and reduce both stock counts.</p>
  <p><code>INSERT</code> adds rows. <code>UPDATE</code> changes values in existing rows. <code>DELETE</code> removes rows. If each change takes effect separately, a failure can leave reduced stock without an order, or an order without its lines.</p>
  <p>A <em>transaction</em> groups changes into one all-or-nothing operation. <code>BEGIN</code> starts it, <code>COMMIT</code> accepts its changes together, and <code>ROLLBACK</code> abandons them. This all-or-nothing property is called <em>atomicity</em>. Follow the successful purchase, then choose the failure after the mug change.</p>
  <ShopFigure kind="purchase" number={3} />
  <p>During the transaction, its own commands can use the changes it has made. Other ordinary PostgreSQL readers cannot see those uncommitted changes. A reader sees a committed state rather than an order halfway through being built; another writer may have to wait.</p>
  <p>The successful purchase ends with P7 stock 0, P3 stock 10 and P9 stock 4. Order 103 has two lines worth 29.00 in total. Nothing in that transaction recorded a payment, so its status stays pending. A payment is a further business event.</p>
  <h3 id="conditions">Conditions on a change</h3>
  <p>Subtracting a mug is safe only if one is available. The condition belongs in the change itself:</p>
  <Sql label="Subtract a mug only when one is available" code={`UPDATE products
SET stock = stock - 1
WHERE product_id = 'P7' AND stock >= 1;`} />
  <p>This command finds P7, requires its stock to be at least 1 and subtracts 1 from that row's current stock. The database reports how many rows changed. One means the mug was allocated by this operation. Zero means its precondition failed: no matching product with enough stock was changed.</p>
  <p>Zero changed rows is not a database error. The caller must recognise it as a failed purchase and roll back the transaction, including any earlier changes. It must check the tea change too, with a required quantity of 2. <a href="/learn/databases/relational/updates-and-imports/">Updates, upserts and bulk imports</a> develops guarded writes and their results.</p>
  <h3 id="buyers">Two buyers and one mug</h3>
  <p>Now let Tariq try to buy the same last mug in purchase 104. Both requests read stock 1. Suppose each calculates the new stock in application code, then writes <code>stock = 0</code>. Putting each purchase inside a transaction does not repair that decision.</p>
  <p>The next drawing runs that mistake, then the guarded change. Read down each buyer's column. In the guarded version, watch what happens to Tariq's condition after Mina commits.</p>
  <ShopFigure kind="race" number={4} />
  <p>The mistaken version can keep both purchases and leave stock at zero. Neither transaction was partial, and the non-negative stock constraint was never broken. The problem was deciding from an earlier read and later writing the calculated value.</p>
  <p>With the guarded subtraction, Mina changes P7 while Tariq waits. At PostgreSQL's <em>Read Committed</em> isolation level, Tariq's update checks its condition again against Mina's committed row. Stock is zero, so it changes no row. Tariq's caller then rolls back purchase 104.</p>
  <p><em>Isolation</em> describes how transactions interact while they run together. The drawing fixes one order of events and one isolation level; another system or level may abort a competing transaction and require a retry. A rule involving several rows can need a different protocol altogether. <code>BEGIN</code> chooses a boundary, but does not by itself make every concurrent decision sound. <a href="/learn/databases/relational/transaction-boundaries/">Transactions and isolation</a> starts from the rule that must survive.</p>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="application">
  <h2 id="application">SQL in an application</h2>
  <p>Mina's browser asks the shop application to place an order. The application uses a database <em>connection</em>, a conversation with the database server, to send the SQL and receive results. The server manages the shared tables; it does not know that a web page is waiting unless the application tells it.</p>
  <figure class="ri-boundary"><figcaption>Figure 5 · One request crosses the application boundary</figcaption><div><strong>Mina's browser</strong><span>Purchase request ↓</span><strong>Shop application</strong><span>One connection: BEGIN → changes → COMMIT ↓</span><strong>Database server</strong><span>Rows changed, results and errors ↑</span></div><p>The application holds the purchase's database conversation together and decides what to tell Mina.</p></figure>
  <p>The commands of one transaction must use the same connection. A connection pool lends connections to requests, but taking a different one for each command would split the conversation. The application must supply values, inspect the result of each change and end the transaction after success or failure.</p>
  <p>Values should travel separately from the SQL structure. In PostgreSQL a parameterised version of Mina's lookup can use <code>WHERE customer_id = $1</code>, with the value <code>1</code> supplied through the driver. The parameter is data; it is not pasted into the command as SQL. The driver is the library that manages this communication for the application.</p>
  <p>An object-relational mapper, or <em>ORM</em>, lets application code work through objects and methods while it constructs SQL. It still sends commands, returns result rows and uses connections. Loading a customer's orders in a loop can still send one query per customer. <a href="/learn/databases/relational/application-sql/">SQL at the application boundary</a> follows these responsibilities through drivers, pools and ORMs.</p>
  <p>The transaction covers its database changes. If the application also charges an external payment provider or sends an email, <code>ROLLBACK</code> cannot uncharge the card or unsend the email. Those actions need their own failure and recovery design. <a href="/learn/databases/relational/outboxes-and-jobs/">Outboxes and database-backed jobs</a> develops a way to record the intention in the transaction and carry it out afterwards.</p>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="work">
  <h2 id="work">The work behind a query</h2>
  <p>Mina's order lookup returns two rows. That says how big the answer is, not how much work produced it. Without a suitable shortcut, the database can read every order and test its customer identifier, discarding all the others. This is a <em>table scan</em>.</p>
  <p>An <em>index</em> is a maintained structure that helps find rows by their values. An index on <code>orders.customer_id</code> can put Mina's entries together and lead to her orders. Compare the routes through the same three shop orders.</p>
  <ShopFigure kind="index" number={6} />
  <p>On a large table, returning two orders by scanning millions of unrelated ones is expensive. The index can reduce that search, but each new order must also add an index entry, and relevant changes must maintain it. More indexes mean more stored data and more work on writes.</p>
  <p>The database's <em>planner</em> chooses the work it expects will be cheapest. A <em>query plan</em> records that choice: which tables to scan, which indexes to use, and how to join or sort their rows. For a table as small as our drawing, reading everything can be cheaper than using an index. Having an index does not compel the planner to use it.</p>
  <p>When a query is slow, inspect the work as well as its answer. <a href="/learn/databases/relational/query-execution/">Query execution and performance</a> follows rows through a plan; <a href="/learn/databases/relational/indexes/">Indexes and access paths</a> develops indexes for the questions an application actually asks.</p>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="durability">
  <h2 id="durability">Keeping a confirmed change</h2>
  <p>Atomicity prevents a partial purchase. <em>Durability</em> means an accepted commit survives a crash. The server cannot keep that promise if the only copy of the purchase is in memory, because a power cut empties memory.</p>
  <p>PostgreSQL records changes in a recovery log on durable storage. With normal durable commit settings, it waits until the log includes a durable commit record before replying successfully. The table files can be written later: after a crash, the log can restore changes they are missing. The storage must keep what it reports as safely written.</p>
  <p>The application receiving that reply is a later event. Compare a crash before the commit becomes durable with one just after it. Watch the recovered purchase and the application's knowledge separately.</p>
  <ShopFigure kind="recovery" number={7} />
  <p>The application saw a dropped connection in both cases. In one, the purchase does not exist; in the other, it does. A missing reply cannot tell it which happened. Retrying as a new order could make a second purchase. <a href="/learn/databases/relational/conflicts-and-retries/">Retries and uncertain commit outcomes</a> develops how to recover one logical request.</p>
  <p>Crash recovery uses the files and log that survived. It cannot rescue a lost disk or decide that a successful deletion was a mistake. A <em>replica</em> supplies another live copy, but it also copies that deletion. A <em>backup</em> preserves a recoverable earlier copy; retained logs can help restore it to a later chosen point. Each protects against a different loss.</p>
  <p><a href="/learn/databases/relational/storage-and-durability/">Storage and durability</a> connects the rows to memory, files and the log. <a href="/learn/databases/relational/replicas-and-read-routing/">Replicas, lag and read routing</a> and <a href="/learn/databases/relational/backup-and-restore/">Backup, restore and reconciliation</a> follow the copies and their limits.</p>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="operating">
  <h2 id="operating">Operating a changing database</h2>
  <p>Suppose Mina's next purchase takes several seconds. Its SQL might be fast once it runs. The delay can occur before the application gets a connection, while the transaction waits for another writer, or while a query does unnecessary work.</p>
  <div class="table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="Where a slow purchase can spend its time"><table><caption>Follow the request to the place where it waits</caption><thead><tr><th>Where the time goes</th><th>What to investigate</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Waiting for a connection</td><td>How many requests are admitted and how long they keep connections</td></tr><tr><td>Waiting behind another transaction</td><td>Who holds the row and why that transaction is still open</td></tr><tr><td>Running the query</td><td>The plan, rows visited and data moved to produce the answer</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
  <p>Making the query faster will not fix an exhausted connection pool. Adding connections can make a busy server compete for the same resources. <a href="/learn/databases/relational/slow-queries/">Diagnosing a database incident</a> starts with evidence that separates these cases.</p>
  <p>The schema changes too. Adding a required column while old application code is still writing is a deployment problem as well as a table definition. The application needs permission to do its job; an account that can read orders need not be allowed to erase them. Backups need a tested restore, and the server needs enough storage for its data and ongoing work. <a href="/learn/databases/relational/schema-changes/">Live schema changes and backfills</a>, <a href="/learn/databases/relational/access-control/">Database access and least privilege</a> and <a href="/learn/databases/relational/connections-and-capacity/">Connections, overload and capacity</a> take those decisions further.</p>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="machines">
  <h2 id="machines">More than one machine</h2>
  <p>Relational tables and SQL do not require all the data to live on one server. There are two different reasons to add machines: keep another copy of the same facts, or divide the facts so different machines do different work.</p>
  <p>A replica is a copy. A <em>shard</em> is a portion of the data. Switch the drawing from copies to portions, then follow purchase 103 across the boundary.</p>
  <ShopFigure kind="placement" number={8} />
  <p>Dividing the data can spread storage and work, but the purchase still needs its mug and tea changes to succeed together. When they live in different portions, that decision requires communication and coordination across machines. A machine or network failure can leave the participants unable to finish immediately.</p>
  <p>A <em>distributed SQL</em> database carries these responsibilities behind a relational interface. A system assembled by an application may leave more of them to its callers. Either way, adding servers does not create another last mug: every purchase of P7 still competes over that fact. A rule covering the whole shop can still need coordination across the whole shop. <a href="/learn/databases/relational/partitioning-and-sharding/">Sharding and distributed SQL</a> derives the costs from what each request must touch.</p>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="implementations">
  <h2 id="implementations">PostgreSQL and CockroachDB</h2>
  <p>The concepts so far apply to several database systems. PostgreSQL and CockroachDB provide two implementation routes through this collection. They accept familiar SQL, but that resemblance does not make every type, concurrent outcome or operating procedure identical.</p>
  <h3 id="postgresql">Inside PostgreSQL</h3>
  <p>A PostgreSQL connection is handled by a server process, working with shared memory and the server's database files. Tables and indexes are managed there; replicas can copy the server's changes to other machines. The guarded-stock example used its Read Committed behaviour. If that is the system your application uses, <a href="/learn/databases/relational/postgresql/architecture/">Inside PostgreSQL</a> follows a request into its processes, pages and maintenance work.</p>
  <h3 id="cockroachdb">Inside CockroachDB</h3>
  <p>A CockroachDB connection reaches a node in a cluster. The system turns table and index operations into work on portions of ordered data called <em>ranges</em>, routes that work to their owners and keeps replicas of the ranges. Transactions can touch several ranges. If distribution, placement or transaction retries affect your application, <a href="/learn/databases/relational/cockroachdb/keys-and-ranges/">Inside CockroachDB</a> follows those boundaries.</p>
  <p>The practical choice starts with the application and its required deployment: where requests and data need to be, which failures must be survived, and who will operate it. Familiar SQL can make a move easier, but the purchase and its failure handling still need checking on the chosen system.</p>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="next">
  <h2 id="next">Choosing a next question</h2>
  <p>A relational system is a useful fit when the application has related facts, several questions over the same records and rules that must hold across different writers. The shop benefits because customer identity, agreed order prices, available stock and payments stay meaningful as the questions change.</p>
  <p>That comes with work: deciding the schema, evolving it while applications run, choosing safe transaction protocols and operating durable copies. SQL does not make a poor row definition accurate, or an unsound concurrent decision safe.</p>
  <p>Choose the next article from the task in front of you. For a new schema, start with <a href="/learn/databases/relational/modelling/">Relational data modelling</a>. For a report that counts too much, follow <a href="/learn/databases/relational/query-results/">Joins and the meaning of a result row</a>. For a purchase that must survive competing callers, use <a href="/learn/databases/relational/transaction-boundaries/">Transactions and isolation</a>. For a slow request, start at <a href="/learn/databases/relational/slow-queries/">Diagnosing a database incident</a>; for lost or unwanted data, at <a href="/learn/databases/relational/backup-and-restore/">Backup, restore and reconciliation</a>.</p>
  <p>You can enter at the question you already have. The earlier explanations are there when that question needs them.</p>
</section>

